WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.720
Do you like over-the-top space battles? Or do&nbsp;
you prefer for "Star Wars" to open with a joke?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.320
We have our opinions, so here's every&nbsp;
"Star Wars" movie's opening scene ranked.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.400
None of these opening scenes are outright&nbsp;
terrible, but one has to come in last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.240
and unfortunately, that honor goes to the&nbsp;
most recent film, "The Rise of Skywalker."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.880
The most poorly-reviewed of the sequel trilogy,&nbsp;
the opening sequence begins promisingly enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.720
As the starfield fades, we see Supreme&nbsp;
Leader Kylo Ren as he plows through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.480
group of enemy combatants on the trail&nbsp;
of the resurrected Emperor Palpatine.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.120
From there, however, things quickly fall apart as&nbsp;
the sequence reveals the scene's true intention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.960
Ren's sole goal in his slaughter is to find a&nbsp;
tracking device that will take him to the stormy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.000
planet Exegol. It foreshadows the rest of the&nbsp;
film's interminable fetch quest plot structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.200
More importantly, the sequence walks&nbsp;
back not only the plot, character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.040
and even design elements of the previous&nbsp;
film, Rian Johnson's "The Last Jedi," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.800
even director J.J. Abrams' own sequel&nbsp;
trilogy opener, "The Force Awakens."

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.200
The opening scene of "The Phantom Menace"&nbsp;
thrusts Jedi Knight Qui-Gon Jinn and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.280
young apprentice Obi-Wan Kenobi into a&nbsp;
bureaucratic taxation conflict between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.000
Trade Federation and the Galactic Senate, with&nbsp;
the small planet of Naboo caught in the middle.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
The sequence is not all dry negotiations,&nbsp;
of course. The Trade Federation, under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.160
command of a mysterious cloaked figure, sends&nbsp;
droid soldiers to kill Qui-Gon and Obi-Wan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.800
and a blaster against lightsaber battle&nbsp;
ensues. But many of Lucas' most questionable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.880
choices in the film, and indeed the prequel&nbsp;
trilogy as a whole, are already present.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.120
We see the flat direction and stilted dialogue&nbsp;
that defeats normally charismatic actors like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.200
Liam Neeson and Ewan McGregor. Then there&nbsp;
is the decision to model several alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.680
species after specific ethnic caricatures. And of&nbsp;
course, there's the preference for lightweight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.840
frictionless CGI over the more tactile,&nbsp;
familiar textures of models and makeup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.080
Every "Star Wars" opening scene works best as a&nbsp;
preview of the style and thematic concerns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.480
the rest of the film will follow, and "Phantom&nbsp;
Menace," for better or worse, is exactly that.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.360
The last of the original trilogy premiered&nbsp;
in 1983, and "Return of the Jedi" is a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.880
whose structure bears more than a little&nbsp;
resemblance to the first "Star Wars" film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.200
As in the original film, "Jedi" spends much&nbsp;
of its first act on the desert planet Tatooine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.120
and climaxes with the Rebels' victory over&nbsp;
the Empire's newly constructed Death Star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.360
One gets the sense that Lucas wanted a do-over on&nbsp;
certain parts of "A New Hope," as if he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.800
take advantage of the technological and budgetary&nbsp;
leaps made since 1977. He effectively re-does the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.760
Mos Eisley cantina scene with a far more complex&nbsp;
menagerie of aliens in Jabba the Hutt's palace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.080
And he stages a bigger, busier Death Star&nbsp;
battle than was possible the first time around.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.520
"Jedi's" opening scene establishes a&nbsp;
new, under-construction Death Star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.520
It also establishes that Darth Vader is&nbsp;
back in a role as a subordinate as he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.680
with Grand Moff Tarkin in "A New Hope." As&nbsp;
he arrives on the Death Star, Vader reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.520
to the commander that the Emperor will be&nbsp;
coming to oversee the completion of the base.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.160
It's an ominous scene that sets up the climactic&nbsp;
stakes of the last film in the original trilogy.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.840
"That is correct, commander."

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.200
"Star Wars" was risking serious&nbsp;
audience fatigue when it released "Solo:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.800
A Star Wars Story" less than six months&nbsp;
after "The Last Jedi." Not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.400
but the production went through a high-profile&nbsp;
shakeup when original directors Phil Lord and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.760
Chris Miller were fired and replaced by filmmaker&nbsp;
Ron Howard, resulting in well-publicized reshoots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.680
If that weren't enough, the film was&nbsp;
attempting to recast Harrison Ford's Han Solo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.280
one of the most iconic pairings of&nbsp;
actor and character in film history.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.240
The resulting film is torn between&nbsp;
delivering a fun space adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.280
and hitting every recognizable&nbsp;
part of Han Solo's backstory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.520
It bounces from important moments like meeting&nbsp;
his Wookiee co-pilot Chewbacca and winning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.720
Millennium Falcon from frenemy Lando Calrissian&nbsp;
to trivial beats like how he got his blaster.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.760
In the opening scene, Howard directs a&nbsp;
speeder chase through the broken down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.160
shipyards of young Han's home planet. The&nbsp;
scene is set to a rousing and romantic new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.720
theme from composer John Williams. But it becomes&nbsp;
bogged down by references to thermal detonators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.400
the gold dice that will one day hang in&nbsp;
the Falcon's cockpit, and the laughably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.640
unnecessary moment in which Han's clearly&nbsp;
symbolic last name is bestowed upon him.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.840
"Attack of the Clones" opens with an explosion&nbsp;
that rocks queen-turned-senator Padmé Amidala's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.840
ship as it lands on the Republic capital&nbsp;
planet Coruscant. The identity of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.920
bomber and their reasons for wanting Padmé&nbsp;
dead are treated as an important mystery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.480
As a result, Obi-Wan Kenobi plays detective&nbsp;
while his apprentice Anakin Skywalker violates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.920
his Jedi oath by falling in love with&nbsp;
Padmé while serving as her bodyguard.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.280
Intrigue is not a color that "Star Wars" paints&nbsp;
with very often, and it's particularly difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.760
with prequels. Audiences know from the start&nbsp;
that the ultimate culprit is newly-elected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.320
Supreme Chancellor Palpatine, who will use a phony&nbsp;
war to consolidate power on the way to becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.600
Emperor and destroying the Jedi. Still, "Attack&nbsp;
of the Clones" leans into a 1940s detective vibe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.280
even down to a bizarre retro diner&nbsp;
where Kenobi gains a vital clue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.680
But while it's fun to imagine a full-on film&nbsp;
noir "Star Wars," the movie ultimately isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.600
interested in sustaining that vibe, and&nbsp;
"Attack of the Clones" soon returns to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.480
more-is-more aesthetic that marks&nbsp;
the prequel trilogy as a whole.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.760
The second "Star Wars" film produced by&nbsp;
Disney, 2016's bleak prequel "Rogue One:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.160
A Star Wars Story" was beset by reshoots and&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes shake-ups just as "Solo" would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.080
be two years later. But the finished film&nbsp;
hangs together remarkably well as a stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.960
consideration of wartime sacrifice that affords&nbsp;
no last-minute rescue for its doomed Rebel spies.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.400
That tone is set in the film's opening scene, a&nbsp;
flashback in which the pastoral life of young Jyn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.760
Erso and her family is shattered by the arrival of&nbsp;
Imperial troops. Her father Galen was an Imperial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.680
scientist whose moral objections to working&nbsp;
on the Death Star forced him into hiding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.000
But now the Empire, represented by the slimy&nbsp;
Orson Krennic, has come calling once again.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:24.560
"You're a hard man to find,&nbsp;
Galen. But farming? Really?”

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.560
Young Jyn flees to an underground bunker as&nbsp;
her mother Lyra is shot down by Stormtroopers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.040
and Galen is taken away to finish his work on&nbsp;
the deadly space station. The scene effectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.200
lays out the narrative and emotional stakes of&nbsp;
the rest of the film. Though, it's also a prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.200
example of the Disney tendency to have everything&nbsp;
important to a character happen all at once.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.640
After a prequel trilogy's worth of Trade&nbsp;
Federations, separatists, Count Dooku, and General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:52.320
Grievous, J.J. Abrams' 2015 sequel "The Force&nbsp;
Awakens," announces its back-to-basics intentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.960
in the first line of the title crawl. Here, the&nbsp;
original trilogy's characters have become legends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:02.400
and inspirations to the series' new characters.&nbsp;
When ace X-Wing pilot Poe Dameron refers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.520
Leia by her newly established rank of General,&nbsp;
an old Rebel ally smiles warmly and replies,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.040
"The general? To me, she's royalty."

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.440
The opening scene concerns the battle for a star&nbsp;
map that will reveal Luke Skywalker's whereabouts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.360
and it structurally serves to introduce&nbsp;
most of the sequel trilogy's new characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.920
There's Poe, a hotshot pilot whose cavalier&nbsp;
personality gets him into a fair bit of trouble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.880
We then meet dark lord Kylo Ren, who attacks Lor&nbsp;
San Tekka's desert village in search of the star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.760
map. Next, there's conflicted Stormtrooper Finn,&nbsp;
whose first taste of combat leaves him traumatized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.440
and looking for a way out. Then there's BB-8, a&nbsp;
next-gen R2-D2 with more charisma and personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:44.160
than many of the prequel trilogy's human&nbsp;
performers. Only Rey is missing from the sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.840
Just like Luke in "A New Hope," she shows up&nbsp;
after the intense opening action sequence.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.360
After two films of political conspiracies,&nbsp;
2005's "Revenge of the Sith" finally lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.000
viewers in the middle of an actual star war, as&nbsp;
Obi-Wan Kenobi and Anakin Skywalker race to rescue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.400
Chancellor Palpatine from the clutches of Sith&nbsp;
Lord Count Dooku and the four-armed Jedi-killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.280
droid General Grievous. Little do they know,&nbsp;
Palpatine is in fact Dooku's evil master,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.280
and that the kidnapping was staged as part&nbsp;
of his years-long plan to rule the galaxy.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.120
"Star Wars" rarely takes full advantage of&nbsp;
the three-dimensionality of outer space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.880
which makes it a pleasant surprise when the&nbsp;
camera pans down from Obi-Wan and Anakin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.040
solitary interceptors to settle on the&nbsp;
gigantic battle raging just below them.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.880
Hayden Christensen still struggles to&nbsp;
infuse Anakin with much charm or passion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:30.000
but overall the film is invigorated by&nbsp;
being the final entry in the prequel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.920
trilogy. At last, Lucas can show all the&nbsp;
things audiences have been waiting for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.640
from the fall of the Republic&nbsp;
to the rise of Darth Vader.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.040
"Star Wars" has had its share of humor,&nbsp;
often powered by the charisma of its actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.440
but it rarely tries to tell jokes. And among&nbsp;
the types of jokes that a "Star Wars" film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.360
would try to tell, a prank phone call would&nbsp;
certainly be one of the more unexpected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.280
But that's exactly what happens at the&nbsp;
start of Rian Johnson's "The Last Jedi."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:57.760
In the sequence, Poe Dameron stalls for time&nbsp;
while his rocket-boosted X-Wing powers up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.760
He hails the First Order commander, General&nbsp;
Hux, and then pretends not to hear him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:05.600
As Hux blusters about in confusion, one&nbsp;
of his captains has to explain the joke:

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.920
"I believe he's tooling with you, sir."

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:12.000
But Johnson has more on his mind than making&nbsp;
"Star Wars" intentionally funny again, as seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.240
in the rest of the opening scene. It features&nbsp;
an intense battle sequence that culminates in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:20.960
disastrous bombing raid, the first of many defeats&nbsp;
for the Resistance over the course of the film.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.040
Like "Rogue One," "The Last Jedi" is concerned&nbsp;
with what it means to sacrifice oneself for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.680
a cause and how to find hope within hopeless&nbsp;
situations. Johnson works through those themes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.200
in both plot and character, building&nbsp;
a poignant relationship between Poe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.760
the cocky flyboy who's never lost, and Leia,&nbsp;
the hardened pragmatist who's lost too much.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:44.000
In the 1970s and '80s, sequels, particularly&nbsp;
sci-fi sequels, gave audiences more of the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:48.720
but cheaper. However, the James Bond series kept&nbsp;
the franchise fresh by offering interesting new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.360
variations on the central theme with each&nbsp;
new entry. And 1974's "The Godfather Part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.040
II," directed by George Lucas' friend&nbsp;
and collaborator Francis Ford Coppola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.200
delivered a sequel that was both more epic&nbsp;
and more intimate than its predecessor.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:04.960
"The Empire Strikes Back" takes its&nbsp;
cues from both Bond and "The Godfather."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:08.480
It establishes many of the elements that&nbsp;
are essential to every "Star Wars" film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:12.560
that followed, while also being unafraid to&nbsp;
challenge its audience with dark material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:16.560
Its quick-and-dirty opening scene strikes&nbsp;
several opposites from "A New Hope."

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.600
Gone are the brown, dry deserts&nbsp;
of Tatooine. In their place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.640
we see the white frozen wasteland of Hoth. Luke&nbsp;
Skywalker gets a hero's entrance in both films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.080
but this time he's suddenly attacked&nbsp;
and dragged away by a wild wampa.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.400
The stakes are immediately set when a floating&nbsp;
probe droid discovers the Rebel base and Luke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.880
awakens in the frozen cave of the vicious beast&nbsp;
that attacked him. Our heroes are not safe here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.520
and their achievements in the&nbsp;
previous movie cannot save them.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:44.960
After 45 years and nearly a dozen films in the&nbsp;
series, there is no opening scene in "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:49.600
that can match that of "A New Hope" in impact,&nbsp;
spectacle, and pure storytelling potential.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.280
As the film opens, the screen is filled&nbsp;
with the impossibly large shape of a Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:58.160
Destroyer chasing the fleeing Tantive IV blockade&nbsp;
runner. Within minutes we meet Princess Leia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.440
Darth Vader, R2-D2, C-3PO, and even the&nbsp;
ever-present stormtroopers. But really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:07.920
it's that scrappy Rebel blockade runner fleeing&nbsp;
laser bolts as the endless leviathan of a Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.880
Destroyer slowly fills the screen as it pursues&nbsp;
that truly sets the tone for the entire film.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:14.960
Even if audiences hadn't read the opening crawl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:18.080
establishing the broad strokes of the&nbsp;
Rebellion's battle against the Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:21.520
they would be able to know whose side we&nbsp;
are meant to be on from the visuals alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:26.720
The "Star Wars" mythology has grown for decades&nbsp;
across movies, books, toys, comics, and games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.320
but it has never outgrown the iconography&nbsp;
of those first indelible minutes from 1977.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:36.880
